---
import Film from '../../plate/Film.astro';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './style.css';

const formula = (tex: string) => renderFormula(tex, false);

const labels = [
  { id: 'exp-three-halves', tex: String.raw`n^{3/2}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'exp-four-thirds', tex: String.raw`n^{4/3}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'exp-may', tex: String.raw`n^{1.014}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'exp-erdos', tex: String.raw`n^{1+c/\log\log n}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'exp-guess', tex: String.raw`n^{1+o(1)}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'jump-forward', tex: String.raw`d=\tfrac{3+4i}{5}`, blue: true },
  { id: 'jump-reciprocal', tex: String.raw`1/d=\tfrac{3-4i}{5}`, blue: false },
  { id: 'jump-product', tex: String.raw`(z_p-z_q)(w_p-w_q)=1`, blue: false },
  { id: 'jump-ratio', tex: String.raw`\lambda=\tfrac{24+7i}{25}`, blue: true },
];
---

<Film
  tag="uruk-unit-links"
  cues={CUES}
  timeline={TIMELINE}
  description="A film counting equal-length links among points, then comparing possible growth with upper caps on one exponent line. The September claim moves the cap in an unspecified direction. Its proof drawings are schematic, and its status is unreviewed. A full text transcript follows."
  caption="Points linked at exactly one unit: small sets and grids counted, Erdős's rescaled grid, the caps from two circles and from 1984, Valtr's non-round circles, and the claimed proof that lowers the cap by an unnamed amount using the jump d and its reciprocal 1/d."
>
  <Fragment slot="labels">
    {labels.map((label) => <span class:list={['film-label', 'film-label--small', { 'film-label--blue': label.blue }]} data-label={label.id}><span set:html={formula(label.tex)} /></span>)}
  </Fragment>
  <p class="film-method">How the film is made. Every link count is computed from the points' coordinates. Two distinct points are linked only when their Euclidean separation equals the chosen unit. The opening eight points have three links. The square has four, the two triangles five, and the six-by-six square and triangular patches have 60 and 85. During the shear, the two grid directions stay unit length; the third direction reaches that length at the end.</p>
  <p class="film-method">For a square grid of k-by-k points and integer squared distance N, the number of unordered links is one half of the sum of (k − |a|)(k − |b|) over all integer pairs satisfying a² + b² = N. The best distances for the displayed sizes are N = 65 at k = 30, giving 4,944 links; N = 325 at k = 60, giving 28,200; and N = 1105 at k = 100, giving 98,176. Counts per point are rounded to two decimal places. The grid rescales the selected distance to one. Thousands of links are drawn in paths grouped by direction.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The exponent line uses one linear scale from 1 to 3/2. The May mark is 1.014, rounded from Sawin's 1.014114 lower exponent along a sequence of sizes. Its label is offset so it remains readable beside 1. Erdős's construction has an exponent tending to 1; its approaching arrow is schematic. The September paper gives no specific improved exponent. Its dashed arrow shows the direction of the claim and has no endpoint tick or numerical value.</p>
  <p class="film-method">The two-circle meetings and unit jumps are exact. The proof's grid uses jumps with integer offsets of squared length 25, divided by five. These include axis directions and 3-4-5 directions. The two arrows are the same oriented link in the coordinates z = x + iy and w = x − iy. Its z jump is d, and its w jump is 1/d, the conjugate of d because its modulus is one. Reversing the geometric link would instead give −d in z. The example ratio of (3 + 4i)/5 to (4 + 3i)/5 is (24 + 7i)/25, also of modulus one. The displayed ratio only illustrates division of two unit jumps; it does not model the proof's asymptotically large heights. Arithmetic height measures number-theoretic complexity and differs from complex absolute value: the displayed jumps and ratio all have modulus one. The rectangle drawing records the alternating-product pattern among four retained endpoint pairs p–r, p–r′, p′–r and p′–r′ in the pair graph. The points need not make a Euclidean rectangle. Its height bars and product comparison are schematic.</p>
  <p class="film-method">Valtr's non-round curve, Székely's crossing picture, the sampled wedges and their information, heights, and the rectangle obstruction are schematic. The film does not reproduce the May number-field construction or verify the September proof. Review statements follow the article's dated evidence. Verification of the May construction concerns a separate lower-bound proof.</p>
</Film>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
